RN .tracking/architecture/code-registry.sysml:49: ceSyncIntegration.controlActions is a 4-element sequence — confirm the schema declares this feature multi-valued ([*]); a sequence in a single-valued attribute is accepted silently today (issue101) WARN .tracking/architecture/code-registry.sysml:74: ceGuards.controlActions is a 2-element sequence — confirm the schema declares this feature multi-valued ([*]); a sequence in a single-valued attribute is accepted silently today (issue101) [guard:sequence-multiplicity] PASS — 2 scanned, 2 warning(s), 0 violation(s) WARN 7 statement(s) across 764 file(s) are SKIPPED by keel-parser and therefore invisible to every guard and view: succession×7. A base-first conversion onto any of these would parse clean and lose its edges (issue102/D0139) [guard:parser-coverage] PASS — 764 scanned, 1 warning(s), 0 violation(s) [guard:base-first-justification] PASS — 0 scanned, 0 warning(s), 0 violation(s) [guard:edge-endpoints] PASS — 10491 scanned, 0 warning(s), 0 violation(s) [guard:ownership] PASS — 0 scanned, 0 warning(s), 0 violation(s) [guard:attestation-authority] PASS — 188 scanned, 0 warning(s), 0 violation(s) [guard:type-collision] PASS — 451 scanned, 0 warning(s), 0 violation(s) [guard:attribute-vocabulary] PASS — 63919 scanned, 0 warning(s), 0 violation(s) [guard:resolver-kind] PASS — 261 scanned, 0 warning(s), 0 violation(s) [guard:stale-gate-prose] PASS — 217 scanned, 0 warning(s), 0 violation(s) [guard:impossible-evidence-date] PASS — 5007 scanned, 0 warning(s), 0 violation(s) [guard:identity-present] PASS — 11260 scanned, 0 warning(s), 0 violation(s) [guard:identity-well-formed] PASS — 11695 scanned, 0 warning(s), 0 violation(s) [guard:tool-reference] PASS — 7 scanned, 0 warning(s), 0 violation(s) [guard:scaffold-placeholder] PASS — 764 scanned, 0 warning(s), 0 violation(s) [guard:claude-surface-drift] PASS — 39 scanned, 0 warning(s), 0 violation(s) WARN d0201 (accepted 2026-08-23): an accepted #ProspectiveChange Decision with NO inbound tracked-item edge — it promises process change but charters no work (D0188). Add a #CharteredBy/#DerivedFrom/#Resolves edge from the item that delivers it, or record why none is needed. WARN d0202 (accepted 2026-08-23): an accepted #ProspectiveChange Decision with NO inbound tracked-item edge — it promises process change but charters no work (D0188). Add a #CharteredBy/#DerivedFrom/#Resolves edge from the item that delivers it, or record why none is needed. [guard:decision-scaffolding] PASS — 24 scanned, 2 warning(s), 0 violation(s) [guard:release-recorded] PASS — 5 scanned, 0 warning(s), 0 violation(s) [guard:enrollment-binding] PASS — 1 scanned, 0 warning(s), 0 violation(s) [guard:control-event-coverage] PASS — 16 scanned, 0 warning(s), 0 violation(s) [guard:question-coverage] PASS — 7 scanned, 0 warning(s), 0 violation(s) [guard:claim-ancestry] PASS — 1 scanned, 0 warning(s), 0 violation(s) [guard:judgment-request-quality] PASS — 0 scanned, 0 warning(s), 0 violation(s) [guard] ALL PASS — 79 warning(s) across 9 guard(s), NOT violations and NOT blocking, but UNREAD until someone reads them: actors, ceremony, viewpoint-renderer, manifest-coverage, attestation-substance, decision-requirement-link, sequence-multiplicity, parser-coverage, decision-scaffolding printed the bare words [guard] ALL PASS while emitting 80 warnings across 10 guards, and one of those warnings was itself a live report-vs-truth contradiction that had shipped and gone unread - 'tag v0.2.0 points at e2c812f but its Release item records commit 425145d'. I read ALL PASS and skated past those warnings repeatedly in the same session, including in commits whose messages claimed the gate was clean. The panel explicitly REFUSED to add a new drift detector on the grounds that this engine already detects more than it triages, so another detector makes the felt problem worse; the fix had to be subtractive. Investigating the specific warning surfaced a second defect: the release-recorded guard did not honor #Supersede, and superseding is the ONLY correction path available to a non-owner (D0108), so that warning was unresolvable by construction - permanent noise that trained me to ignore the whole class.";
        :>> decision = "TWO changes, both subtractive. (1) The guard verdict line STATES ITS WARNING POPULATION: instead of the bare word ALL PASS, it reads 'ALL PASS - N warning(s) across M guard(s), NOT violations and NOT blocking, but UNREAD until someone reads them: <guard names>'. No new surface, no new computation, no new cost - the number appears where the verdict is already read, so ALL PASS can no longer be read without also reading how much went unread. (2) The release-recorded guard EXCLUDES a Release carrying an inbound #Supersede edge, matching what the sibling views already do for superseded Needs (issue088), requirements (issue127) and tasks (issue100). A guard whose warning cannot be cleared through the engine's own sanctioned correction mechanism is a guard that will warn forever. NOT DONE and deliberately so: no new detector, no new gate, no promotion of any warning to blocking - D0098 keeps completeness off the commit path and the panel's argument is that the marginal detector now subtracts value.";
        :>> rationale = "Detected-but-unread grows with every control added, which makes it the one drift mechanism that gets WORSE as assurance work proceeds - the opposite of the usual assumption. Stating the population at the point of the verdict is the honest-surface shape (N-C2) applied to the loudest surface in the engine: a bare pass hid a measured number. The supersede fix is the same class as three already-accepted fixes, so it is consistency rather than novelty, and it is what makes the first change actionable instead of merely louder - a warning population that CANNOT go down is noise no matter how prominently it is printed.";
        :>> consequences = "COUNTS MOVED and both sides are reported, as issue127's precedent requires: release-recorded went 5 scanned / 1 warning -> 5 scanned / 0 warnings, and the whole-suite total went 82 warnings across 11 guards -> 79 across 9. The extra delta is two priority-inversion warnings that this same work cleared honestly: appending two follow-on items above a High-issue resolver had silently re-prioritized them, the guard caught it, and the backlog was reordered rather than the warning explained away. The v0.2.0 tag disagreement is triaged rather than suppressed: git shows the recorded commit IS an ancestor of the tag two commits back, so the record was true when written and the tag was cut later; the tag is published and must not move (D0129), and release020 is owned by another actor, so a new release020Shipped records the shipped commit and SUPERSEDES the field - the sanctioned non-owner correction. Resolves issue244.";
    }

